  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Unsettled weather will continue through the first \nhalf of Saturday with occasional showers, and a few wet snowflakes \nacross higher terrain.\n\nA deep, nearly vertically stacked low over NY this afternoon will \nmove northeast across northern New England tonight, reaching the \nCanadian Maritimes Saturday. Forcing for ascent from the passing \nsystem combined with a large region of deep moisture will continue \nto support occasional showers through the first half of Saturday. \nThe airmass will grow cold enough to support lake enhancement as \nwell. A highly sheared environment today due to the close proximity \nof the upper level low will give way to well aligned westerly flow \ntonight through Saturday morning, allowing for lake enhancement and \nupslope enhancement east of the lakes.\n\nThe airmass is not very cold, with 850MB temps bottoming out around\n-2C tonight. This will only allow for a few wet snowflakes to mix in \nat times across the highest elevations, with no accumulation \nexpected. Otherwise, rain will continue to be the predominant precip \ntype for lower elevations. The greatest additional rainfall amounts \nwill be found in lake enhanced/upslope areas across the Chautauqua \nRidge, Boston Hills, and Tug Hill Plateau. Additional rainfall in \nthese areas may reach 0.25\" to 0.50\", with less than 0.25\" \nelsewhere.\n\nDeep moisture and forcing will exit from west to east Saturday \nafternoon and evening, with showers ending from west to east. The \nafternoon will become dry in Western NY, with the last of the \nshowers exiting the North Country by late evening.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A brief period of dry weather will return late \nSaturday through most of Sunday. \n\nA narrow ridge of high pressure surface and aloft will briefly build \ninto the eastern Great Lakes Saturday night through early Sunday, \nsupporting a short break in the active weather. The ridge axis will \ndrift off the east coast Sunday afternoon, with the next strong \ntrough moving into the western Great Lakes. Moisture transport and \nforcing for ascent will increase late in the day, with showers \nreaching Western NY by late afternoon or early evening.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Another area of widespread rain will cross the \nregion Sunday evening through early Monday, with a few leftover rain \nand snow showers Monday night through Tuesday morning.\n\nA northern and southern stream shortwave will phase over the western \nGreat Lakes Sunday, with the sharp trough reaching the eastern Great \nLakes by Monday morning. A deepening surface low will cross the Ohio \nValley Sunday evening, then reach New England Monday. Another period \nof strong ascent and moisture transport will cross the eastern Great \nLakes Sunday evening through Monday morning, supporting widespread \nrain across the region. While forcing is strong, the system will \nmove quickly, keeping rainfall totals lower than yesterday. \nPrecipitation type will be all rain through Monday morning.\n\nA brief push of colder air will move into the eastern Great Lakes \nbehind this system, with 850MB temps bottoming out around -8C Monday \nnight. Despite the cold air aloft, short fetch northwest flow and \nrapidly diminishing synoptic scale moisture will keep the lake \nresponse muted. Expect some rain and snow showers southeast of the \nlakes late Monday through early Tuesday, but any accumulation will \nbe spotty and minor.",
